  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Authorization applies everywhere",
      "items": [
        "A route practiceId, stale client role or hidden button grants nothing. Every read/write/job/source/file/export resolves current capability and ownership.",
        "Administrator has no implicit clinical access. Scheduler sees minimum identity/contact; registration needs both SCHEDULE_MANAGE and PATIENT_REGISTER. Patient gets only their record link and curated publications."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Session and device decisions — ADR-008 accepted",
      "items": [
        "Web/PWA: secure host-only opaque cookie and CSRF, no bearer in browser storage. Native: same opaque server session over the Authorization header from an exact allowlisted origin; platform secure storage only.",
        "No mixed transports; bearer is rejected from web origins. Revoke on logout, reset and suspension; device registration can be revoked. Native duration and exact origin/framework support remain story 10.5 checks."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Contract and client compatibility — ADR-009 accepted",
      "items": [
        "One OpenAPI source and generated TypeScript client; additive /api/v1 evolution, X-Client-Version and minimum-version policy; outdated clients show an update screen.",
        "96 operations are currently declared. getApiMeta and four native/device operations remain planned contract additions; CLIENT_TOO_OLD / 426 is not yet declared. Compatibility window numbers remain an assumption."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Device and database limits",
      "items": [
        "The service worker caches the application shell only; no clinical cache at rest. Push is content-free, reauthorized at delivery and revoked with device access. Offline clinical mode, native practitioner UI and biometric controls are deferred.",
        "Identity/bootstrap lookup and job discovery use narrow metadata access, then establish context. Domain queries use the same physical connection and transaction-local RLS; migration credentials never reach HTTP runtime. RLS alone is not clinical authorization."
      ]
    }
  ]
